  "about": "The gospel of the Fourth Sunday of Advent. Luke dates the year by seven offices at once — an emperor, a governor, three tetrarchs and two high priests — and only then says the word of God came to a man who holds none of them.",
  "analysis_defaults": { "confidence": "high", "sources": ["editorial"], "review": "pending" },
  "segments": {
    "s01": { "translation": "In the fifteenth year of the reign of Tiberius Caesar, Pontius Pilate being governor of Judea, and Herod being tetrarch of Galilee, and Philip his brother tetrarch of Iturea and the country of Trachonitis, and Lysanias tetrarch of Abilene, under the high priests Annas and Caiphas, the word of the Lord came to John, the son of Zachary, in the desert.", "translation_citations": [{ "title": "Biblia Sacra Vulgata", "locator": "Luke 3:1-2" }] },
    "s02": { "translation": "And he came into all the country about the Jordan, preaching the baptism of penance for the remission of sins, as it is written in the book of the sayings of Isaiah the prophet: A voice of one crying in the wilderness: Prepare ye the way of the Lord, make straight His paths. Every valley shall be filled, and every mountain and hill shall be brought low: and the crooked shall be made straight, and the rough ways plain: and all flesh shall see the salvation of God.", "translation_citations": [{ "title": "Biblia Sacra Vulgata", "locator": "Luke 3:3-6" }] }
  },
